60611"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"99156373917403941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.8 using\n\t\t\t\t\tMARC21slim_MODS3-8_XSL2-0.xsl (Revision 2.79 2025/03/28)","languageOfCataloging":{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}}}},{"titleInfo":{"title":"Marcus Aurelius"},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Sellars, John",{"@type":"date","#text":"1971-"}],"role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author."}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2021"},"copyrightDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2021"},"issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Abingdon, Oxon ;"}},"publisher":"Routledge,","dateIssued":"2021."}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"x, 146 pages ; 25 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"In this new study, John Sellars offers a fresh examination of Marcus Aurelius' Meditations as a work of philosophy by placing it against the background of the tradition of Stoic philosophy to which Marcus was committed. The Meditations of Marcus Aurelius is a perennial bestseller, attracting countless readers drawn to its unique mix of philosophical reflection and practical advice. The emperor is usually placed alongside Seneca and Epictetus as one of three great Roman Stoic authors, but he wears his philosophy lightly, not feeling the need to state explicitly the ideas standing behind the reflections that he was writing for himself. As a consequence, his standing as a philosopher has often been questioned. Challenging claims that Marcus Aurelius was merely an eclectic thinker, that the Meditations do not fit the model of a work of philosophy, that there are no arguments in the work, and that it only contains superficial moral advice, Sellars shows that he was in constant dialogue with his Stoic predecessors, engaging with themes drawn from all three parts of Stoicism, logic, physics, and ethics. The image of Marcus Aurelius that emerges is of a committed Stoic, engaging with a wide range of philosophical topics, motivated by the desire to live a good life. This volume will be of interest to scholars and students of both Classics and Philosophy\"--"},"tableOfContents":"Part I Marcus and his Meditations p. 5 -- 1 Marcus the Stoic philosopher p. 7 -- 2 The Meditations, a philosophical text p. 20 -- Part II Logic p. 37 -- 3 Impressions and judgements p. 39 -- Part III Physics p. 55 -- 4 Nature and change p. 57 -- 5 Fate and providence p. 68 -- 6 Soul and emotion p. 81 -- 7 Time and death p. 90 -- 8 Virtue and justice p. 107 -- 9 The cosmic city p. 117.","note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"John Sellars."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (pages [130]-137) and index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Marcus Aurelius",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Emperor of Rome"},{"@type":"date","#text":"121-180"}]},"titleInfo":{"title":"Meditations"}},{"@authority":"cct","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Marcus Aurelius",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Emperor of Rome"},{"@type":"date","#text":"121-180"}]}},{"@authority":"gnd","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Mark Aurel",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Römisches Reich, Kaiser"},{"@type":"date","#text":"121-180"}]},"titleInfo":{"title":"Ad se ipsum"}},{"@authority":"nli","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Marcus Aurelius",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Emperor of Rome"},{"@type":"date","#text":"121-180"}]},"titleInfo":{"title":"Meditations"}},{"@authority":"ram","name":{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Marc Aurèle",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"empereur romain"},{"@type":"date","#text":"0121-0180"}]},"titleInfo":{"title":"Pensées"}},{"@authority":"fast","titleInfo":{"title":"Meditations (Marcus Aurelius, Emperor of Rome)"}},{"@authority":"cct","topic":"Stoicism"},{"@authority":"ram","topic":"Stoïcisme"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"B583 .S455 2021"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"188"},{"@authority":"clc","#text":"B502.43"}],"relatedItem":[{"titleInfo":{"title":"Philosophy in the Roman world"}},{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Marcus aurelius"},"name":{"namePart":"Sellars, John,"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"New York : Routledge, 2020."},"identifier":{"@type":"local","#text":"(DLC)  2020006227"}},{"@type":"series","titleInfo":{"title":"Philosophy in the Roman world"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99155238699103941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780367146061"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0367146061"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9780367146078"},{"@type":"isbn","#text":"036714607X"},{"@type":"isbn","@invalid":"yes","#text":"9780429052651"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"2020006226"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"1140357458"}],"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000460635171","#text":"Center for Hellenic Studies Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"B583 .S455 2021"},null,null],"extension":[{"librarycloud":{"HarvardRepositories":{"HarvardRepository":"Ctr Hellen Studies"},"originalDocument":"https://s3.amazonaws.com/harvard.librarycloud.marc/99155238699103941","processingDate":"2024-08-03T08:47Z"}},{"sets":null}],"recordInfo":{"descriptionStandard":"rda","recordContentSource":{"@authority":"marcorg","#text":"DLC"},"recordCreationDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"200206"},"recordChangeDate":{"@encoding":"iso8601","#text":"20240720"},"recordIdentifier":{"@source":"MH:ALMA","#text":"99155238699103941"},"recordOrigin":"Converted from MARCXML to MODS version 3.6 using MARC21slim2MODS3-6.xsl\n        (Revision 1.117 2017/02/14)","languageOfCataloging":{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"}}}},{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":[{"nonSort":{"@space":"preserve","#text":"The  "},"title":"Meditations of Marcus Aurelius Antoninus "},{"@type":"uniform","@nameTitleGroup":"1","title":"Meditations. English"}],"name":[{"@nameTitleGroup":"1","@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Marcus Aurelius,",{"@type":"termsOfAddress","#text":"Emperor of Rome,"},{"@type":"date","#text":"121-180"}],"role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},{"@type":"personal","namePart":["Farquharson, A. S. L. (Arthur Spenser Loat),",{"@type":"date","#text":"1871-1942"}]},{"@type":"personal","namePart":"Rutherford, R. B."}],"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Early works"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Records and correspondence"}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"enk"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"England"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Oxford, England "}},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":{"namePart":"Oxford University Press"},"dateIssued":"1989"},"language":[{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},{"languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"grc"}},{"@objectPart":"translation","languageTerm":{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"grc"}}],"physicalDescription":{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xxv, 195 p. ; 22 cm."},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"Marcus Aurelius governed Rome and its empire from AD161 to 180, and his reign was long remembered as a golden age in which the citizens of that empire enjoyed the gentle rule of a philosophic sage. The truth was of course not so simple. The Meditations of Marcus' old age, composed while on campaign, give us exceptional access to the emperor's mind. Although they are not generally concerned withe the day-to-day business of warfare and administration, they do reveal, however elusively, the personality of the writer: clear-headed, serious, often disillusioned with his own status and with human activities in general. The work can be read as a historical document and as a spiritual diary: its vivid imagery and pungent epigrams have prompted comparisons with Pascal's Pensés and the biblical book of Ecclesiastes. A.S.L. Farquharson's translation was originally published in 1944, as part of a major commentary on Marcus Aurelius' work. In this volume Farquharson's work is brought up to date and supplied with an introduction and notes for students and the general reader. A selection of lively letters from Marcus to his tutor Fronto, most of which date from his earlier years is also included: these provide much more personal detail and help to fill out the sombre and selective portrait of the emperor which we find in the Meditations\"--Unedited summary from book jacket."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"The Meditations of Marcus Aurelius Antoninus -- A selection from the correspondence of Marcus Cornelius Fronto and Marcus Aurelius."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"translated by A.S.L. Farquharson ; and selection from the letters of 